  "label": "TMUA 2022 · Paper 1 · Question 3",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2022 · Paper 1 · Question 3 — Recover a constant second derivative from two function values and a definite integral.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "F",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Undo the two derivatives",
      "body": "<p>Integrate the constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> twice. Keep a new arbitrary constant at each integration.</p>",
      "recap": "Knowing a second derivative determines a quadratic up to a linear term and a constant.",
      "pitfall": "One integration only recovers the first derivative.",
      "pause": "Write the general quadratic expression for the function.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "Integrating a power raises its exponent by one and divides by the new exponent; include a constant for an indefinite integral."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the two point values",
      "body": "<p>Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> supplies two equations for these coefficients.</p>",
      "recap": "Point values provide equations for integration constants.",
      "pitfall": "The coefficient of the square is half the second derivative.",
      "pause": "Use the two point values to remove one constant and relate the remaining two.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-simultaneous-equations",
          "reminder": "Substitution and elimination turn several conditions on one expression into simultaneous equations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the remaining condition",
      "body": "<p>Integrate your quadratic between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and set that result equal to the stated integral. This gives the extra equation needed.</p>",
      "recap": "A definite integral can determine a coefficient as well as an area.",
      "pitfall": "Apply the bounds to the entire antiderivative.",
      "pause": "Solve the two remaining linear equations and check the resulting function against all three conditions.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "Evaluate an antiderivative at the upper endpoint and subtract its value at the lower endpoint."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Integrating <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> twice gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>The integral condition gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Subtracting the two equations yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>As a check, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> has the two stated endpoint values, integral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and constant second derivative <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer F.</strong></p>",
